@page applications_exahype2_landslides Landslides

\tableofcontents

The physical model used to simulate avalanches is based on the Shallow Water Equations (SWE), extended to incorporate the Voellmy-Salm friction model, where
the friction resistance of the granular material is represented as a source term. The Voellmy-Salm model accounts for both velocity-independent Coulomb friction and turbulent-like drag,
which are critical for accurately simulating granular flows.

# Friction Model
The Voellmy-Salm rheological law, initially developed by Voellmy and later extended by Salm for dense granular flows, is widely adopted in avalanche and
landslide modeling. This semi-empirical framework decomposes the basal shear stress \f$ \tau \f$ into
two physically distinct components:

\f[
    \tau = \mu \sigma + \frac{\rho g V^2}{\xi}
\f]

where \f$ \sigma \f$ is the normal stress at the flow base; \f$ \mu \f$ is the basal friction coefficient;
\f$ \rho \f$ is the bulk density of the granular material; \f$ g \f$ is the gravitational acceleration;
\f$ \xi \f$ is the Voellmy turbulence coefficient; and \f$ V \f$ is the velocity vector.

# Shallow Water Equations with Friction as a Source

The SWE augmented with the Friction Law as a source term, forms the governing equations for gravity-driven granular avalanches:

\f{align*}{
        \begin{cases}
            \frac{\partial h}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial (hu)}{\partial x} + \frac{\partial (hv)}{\partial y} = 0 \\
            \frac{\partial (hu)}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial}{\partial x}(hu^2 + \frac{gh^2}{2}) + \frac{\partial (huv)}{\partial y} = -gh \frac{\partial z}{\partial x} - \frac{u}{V}(\mu gh \cos(\alpha)+ g \frac{V^2}{\xi}) \\
            \frac{\partial (hv)}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial (hvu)}{\partial x}+ \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(hv^2 + \frac{gh^2}{2})  = -gh \frac{\partial z}{\partial y} - \frac{v}{V}(\mu gh \cos(\alpha)+ g \frac{V^2}{\xi}) \\
        \end{cases},
\f}

where \f$ V \f$ is the magnitude of the velocity vector. \f$ \alpha \f$ is the local slope angle, \f$ \mu \f$ is the
basal friction coefficient, and \f$ \xi \f$ is the Voellmy turbulence coefficient. \f$ \mu \f$ and \f$ \xi \f$ are asummed constant here.

